<!-- §01 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Validation Ladder</div>
<h2>From idea to confidence — without burning the cluster</h2>
<p>Suppose you have a novel MTP architecture: a Gemma 4-style draft module that co-trains parallel prediction heads alongside the main model, then decodes in a single diffusion-like forward pass. The idea is elegant. But the only way to know if it works at scale is to test it at scale — unless you build a validation ladder first.</p>
<p>Each rung of the ladder is orders of magnitude cheaper than the one above it. The goal is to climb as few rungs as possible while eliminating the hypothesis with confidence. If your idea survives all four rungs at small scale, it earns the right to a full training run.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">validation-ladder.py — cost vs confidence</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">each rung is 100–1000× cheaper than the next</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ladder">
      <div class="rung r1">
        <div class="rung-num">1</div>
        <div class="rung-body">
          <div class="rung-title">Proxy Training — algorithmic task sandbox</div>
          <div class="rung-desc">Train a 30M–100M model on polynomial arithmetic and pattern-copying tasks. Verify that parallel heads learn to plan ahead better than AR baseline. Takes 1–2 hours on a single GPU.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rung-cost">~$5</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rung r2">
        <div class="rung-num">2</div>
        <div class="rung-body">
          <div class="rung-title">Loss Gap Monitoring — short 5K-step trial</div>
          <div class="rung-desc">Run a 5,000-step pre-training trial on the target cluster. Both lm_loss and mtp losses must trend down with a stable gap. Catch gradient spikes and scaling factor pathologies early.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rung-cost">~$200</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rung r3">
        <div class="rung-num">3</div>
        <div class="rung-body">
          <div class="rung-title">Speculative Decoding Scaling Laws — affine fit</div>
          <div class="rung-desc">Train 3–5 small variants, fit α = ax + by + c relating token acceptance rate to draft/target perplexities. Analytically find the throughput-optimal head configuration.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rung-cost">~$2K</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rung r4">
        <div class="rung-num">4</div>
        <div class="rung-body">
          <div class="rung-title">Serving Dynamics Simulation — queuing theory</div>
          <div class="rung-desc">Use Little's Law to find the server load threshold where parallel decoding overhead negates its own latency savings. No new training needed — uses numbers from rungs 1–3.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rung-cost">~$0</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The validation ladder isn't just about saving money — it's about generating evidence in the right order. Each rung answers a different question. Rung 1: <em>can the architecture learn at all?</em> Rung 2: <em>is it stable to train?</em> Rung 3: <em>is the head configuration right?</em> Rung 4: <em>will it actually be faster in production?</em></p>
</section>

<!-- §02 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-proxy" data-code="proxy">
<div class="section-no">02 — Step 1: Proxy Training</div>
<h2>Validate on toy tasks before touching real data</h2>
<p>The first question to answer is simple: do your parallel prediction heads actually learn to pre-plan representations? A 70B model will eventually converge on almost anything — but a 30M model on a carefully designed synthetic task will tell you within hours whether the learning signal is healthy.</p>
<p>The key insight from Meta's MTP paper is that polynomial arithmetic tasks are a near-perfect proxy. To compute the last digit of <code>x1*x2 + x3*x4 - x5</code>, a model must look ahead — it cannot emit the answer token-by-token without knowing what comes next. A standard AR model struggles; a properly-trained MTP model with parallel heads excels because the representation must encode forward planning.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">proxy-training — step-by-step walkthrough</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to advance</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="proxy-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Define the algorithmic task</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Generate polynomial arithmetic examples: random 5-variable polynomials mod 10. The model must predict all answer digits simultaneously in a single pass with K parallel heads.</div>
          <div class="step-code">f(x1,x2,x3,x4,x5) = (x1*x2 + x3*x4 - x5) mod 1000</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Train two models: AR baseline vs MTP (K=4)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Both are 30M transformers. The AR baseline predicts one token at a time. The MTP model has 4 extra prediction heads — one for each lookahead position — trained jointly.</div>
          <div class="step-code">mtp_loss = lm_loss + λ*(head1_loss + head2_loss + ...)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Measure pass@k on the held-out test set</div>
          <div class="step-desc">For each example, check if the model produces the correct digit sequence. pass@1 measures exact match. pass@4 counts any correct answer among 4 samples — directly comparable to AR with 4 attempts.</div>
          <div class="step-code">pass@k = 1 - (1 - p)^k   # unbiased estimator</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Check the gap: MTP should beat AR by ≥5%</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A healthy MTP model on polynomial tasks shows 5–15% higher pass@k than the AR baseline at equal compute. If the gap is negative or near zero, the parallel heads are not learning to plan — investigate before scaling.</div>
          <div class="step-code">assert mtp_pass_k &gt; ar_pass_k * 1.05, "heads not planning"</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="proxy-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="proxy-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="proxy-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Pattern-copying tasks complement polynomial arithmetic: the model sees a sequence of tokens, then must reproduce them in reverse or with a shift — a test for induction heads. If MTP breaks induction head formation, it will hurt in-context learning at scale. Both tasks should pass before proceeding to rung 2.</p>

<div class="callout tip">
  <p><strong>Quick sanity check:</strong> if your parallel heads are learning, the per-head loss at step 1000 should be roughly <code>head_k_loss ≈ lm_loss + 0.3 * k</code> — each lookahead step is harder by about 0.3 nats. A head that converges to the same loss as <code>lm_loss</code> or much higher is a signal of a bug in how the heads are connected.</p>
</div>

<div id="s-proxy-bars" style="margin:1.5rem 0">
  <div style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.875rem;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:600">Polynomial Arithmetic — pass@4 at 30M parameters</div>
