</section>

<!-- §03 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-loss" data-code="loss">
<div class="section-no">03 — Step 2: Loss Gap Monitoring</div>
<h2>Stable gap = healthy training; diverging gap = fix before scaling</h2>
<p>A 5,000-step pre-training trial on your target cluster costs a few hundred dollars and reveals problems that proxy training cannot: gradient spikes, loss divergence, and scaling factor pathologies. The Megatron Bridge documentation identifies one rule that must hold throughout the trial.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Loss gap invariant</div>
  <span class="math-var">lm_loss</span> &gt; <span class="math-var">mtp_1_loss</span> &gt; <span class="math-var">mtp_2_loss</span> &gt; …
  <br>
  <span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.75rem">gap(<span class="math-var">k</span>) = mtp_k_loss − lm_loss ≈ constant throughout training</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">if gap widens → reduce mtp_loss_scaling_factor (target: 0.05–0.2)</span>
</div>

<p>The visualization below shows two training runs. The "stable" run shows healthy behavior: both losses trend down together, and the gap between them remains roughly constant. The "diverging" run shows a pathology: the main language modeling loss improves, but the MTP auxiliary losses explode after step 3,000 — a sign that the scaling factor is too high and the auxiliary task is interfering with the primary objective.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">loss-monitor.canvas — toggle scenario to see the difference</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">interactive toggle</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="scenario-btns">
      <button class="scenario-btn active-good" id="btn-stable" onclick="setScenario('stable')">✓ Stable (good)</button>
      <button class="scenario-btn" id="btn-diverge" onclick="setScenario('diverge')">✗ Diverging (bad)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="loss-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div class="sf-row">
      <span class="sf-label">mtp_loss_scaling_factor:</span>
      <div class="sf-btns">
        <button class="sf-btn" id="sf-005" onclick="setScaleFactor(0.05)">0.05</button>
        <button class="sf-btn active" id="sf-01" onclick="setScaleFactor(0.1)">0.1</button>
        <button class="sf-btn" id="sf-02" onclick="setScaleFactor(0.2)">0.2</button>
        <button class="sf-btn" id="sf-03" onclick="setScaleFactor(0.3)">0.3 ⚠</button>
      </div>
      <span class="sf-note" id="sf-note">recommended range: 0.05–0.2</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The practical fix for divergence is two-pronged: reduce <code>mtp_loss_scaling_factor</code> (start at 0.1) and add gradient clipping (max norm 1.0). If divergence persists even at 0.05, the architecture itself may need revisiting — the auxiliary prediction heads may be competing with rather than complementing the main objective.</p>

<div class="callout warn">
  <p><strong>Watch for NaN losses at step 1–100:</strong> early instability almost always means the parallel heads share an embedding table with the main model but are receiving gradients with the wrong sign convention. Confirm that the head output projection matrices are initialized independently from the main decoder head.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-sdsl" data-code="sdsl">
<div class="section-no">04 — Step 3: Speculative Decoding Scaling Laws</div>
<h2>Predict inference throughput before committing to a head configuration</h2>
<p>Even if your MTP architecture trains stably, the inference speedup depends entirely on one number: the token acceptance rate <strong>α</strong>. If the parallel draft heads are good, α is high and every batch of K tokens is accepted at once. If α is low, the overhead of evaluating K heads per step actually slows things down.</p>
<p>The Speculative Decoding Scaling Laws paper shows that α can be predicted from an affine function of two observable quantities: the draft model's perplexity and the target model's perplexity. You don't need to train the full model to know if your parallel heads will be fast enough — you only need 3–5 small variants to fit the coefficients.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">SDSL acceptance rate formula</div>
  <span class="math-var">α</span> = <span class="math-var">a</span> · <span class="math-var">x</span> + <span class="math-var">b</span> · <span class="math-var">y</span> + <span class="math-var">c</span>
  <br>
  <span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.75rem"><span class="math-var">x</span> = draft head perplexity&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-var">y</span> = target model perplexity</span>
  <br>
  <span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.75rem">fit &#123;a, b, c&#125; from 3–5 small-scale runs; then solve analytically for throughput-optimal K</span>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">sdsl.canvas — predicted acceptance rate vs draft perplexity</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">select target model PPL</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ppl-btns">
      <button class="ppl-btn active" id="ppl-all" onclick="setSdslMode('all')">Show all</button>
      <button class="ppl-btn" id="ppl-5" onclick="setSdslMode('5')">Target PPL 5</button>
      <button class="ppl-btn" id="ppl-10" onclick="setSdslMode('10')">Target PPL 10</button>
      <button class="ppl-btn" id="ppl-20" onclick="setSdslMode('20')">Target PPL 20</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="sdsl-canvas" style="width:100%;height:300px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.55">The dashed line at α = 0.7 marks the <strong style="color:var(--cyan)">throughput-optimal threshold</strong>: below this, parallel decoding overhead exceeds the gain. The intersection point determines the maximum acceptable draft perplexity for a given target model.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The practical implication: before your full training run, train 3–5 mini-variants at 100M parameters with different head sizes (1, 2, 4, 8 lookahead steps). Fit the affine function. If the predicted α for your intended head configuration is below 0.7 at the target model's expected perplexity, shrink the heads or reduce K — the speedup simply won't materialize.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-serving" data-code="serving">
<div class="section-no">05 — Step 4: Serving Dynamics</div>
<h2>Little's Law: find the load threshold where parallel decoding stops paying off</h2>
<p>Even if α is healthy and training is stable, parallel decoding changes the hardware bottlenecks in ways that only matter at production load. A single-request benchmark will always favor parallel decoding. But under high server load — many concurrent requests — the dynamics invert.</p>
<p>The key tool is <strong>Little's Law</strong>: in any stable queuing system, the average number of requests in the system N equals the arrival rate λ times the average latency W.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Little's Law</div>
  <span class="math-var">N</span> = <span class="math-var">λ</span> · <span class="math-var">W</span>
  <br>
  <span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.75rem">infer effective batch size from arrival rate; decompose cost into prefill + parallel draft + verification</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">parallel draft phase saturates GPU HBM bandwidth under high batch load</span>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">serving-dynamics.canvas — per-token latency vs server load</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover to read values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="serving-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.55">
      The <span style="color:var(--purple-l)">purple line</span> shows the MTP parallel decoding latency; the <span style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.4)">grey line</span> shows the AR autoregressive baseline. The crossover point — where MTP latency exceeds AR — is the <strong style="color:var(--amber)">deployment threshold</strong>: beyond this server load, switching back to AR is faster.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The concrete takeaway: for any α you estimated in step 3, you can compute the exact load (in requests per second) at which MTP parallel decoding stops being worth it. If your production load is expected to exceed that threshold, you need either a higher-α head configuration or a hybrid routing strategy that falls back to AR under high load.</p>

<div class="callout tip">
  <p><strong>Practical numbers:</strong> for a 7B model with α = 0.72 and K = 4 parallel heads, the crossover typically occurs around 40–60 concurrent requests per GPU — above this, the 4× memory bandwidth amplification of evaluating K draft tokens simultaneously saturates the HBM bus faster than the latency savings accumulate.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §06 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-checklist" data-code="checklist">
<div class="section-no">06 — The Full Checklist</div>
<h2>What a green light looks like at each rung</h2>
<p>Each validation step has a binary pass/fail criterion. An idea that clears all four can be taken to a full pre-training run with confidence. An idea that fails at rung 1 should not proceed past a sandbox; failing at rung 3 means re-design, not scaling.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">validation-pipeline — walk through each gate</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to advance</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="gate-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Gate 1: Algorithmic proxy tasks pass</div>
          <div class="step-desc">MTP model achieves &gt;5% higher pass@k than AR baseline on polynomial arithmetic at 30M parameters. Pattern-copying (induction heads) is not degraded relative to AR.</div>
          <div class="step-code">PASS: mtp_pass_k &gt; ar_pass_k * 1.05</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Gate 2: Loss gap stable over 5K steps</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Both lm_loss and all mtp_k_losses trend monotonically downward. The gap between them does not widen more than 10% from its value at step 1,000. No NaN losses observed.</div>
          <div class="step-code">PASS: gap(5000) &lt; gap(1000) * 1.10</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Gate 3: Predicted acceptance rate α ≥ 0.7</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Affine fit from 3–5 small variants predicts α ≥ 0.7 at the target model's expected perplexity with the intended head configuration K. If not, reduce K or increase head quality.</div>
          <div class="step-code">PASS: predict_alpha(draft_ppl, target_ppl) &gt;= 0.70</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Gate 4: Serving crossover above expected load</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Little's Law simulation shows MTP parallel decoding stays faster than AR up to at least 120% of expected production load. If crossover is lower, design a fallback routing policy.</div>
          <div class="step-code">PASS: crossover_rps &gt; expected_peak_rps * 1.20</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="gate-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="gate-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="gate-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The validation ladder costs roughly $2,200 in total compute — less than 0.01% of what a failed frontier training run would cost. More importantly, it generates a paper trail: if you publish the architecture, reviewers can verify that all four gates were passed and the speedup claim is grounded in more than a single benchmark.</p>

<div class="callout tip">
  <p><strong>When to skip rungs:</strong> if you are adapting an existing, published MTP variant (e.g., adding Gemma 4-style centroid heads to a validated DeepSeek MTP setup), you may be able to skip rung 1 and go directly to rung 2. But rung 3 (scaling laws) should never be skipped — the optimal K is always architecture-specific, and assumptions from one model family rarely transfer.</p>
</div>
</section>

